  actor: jgstern_agent  # 719d
  clock: 2  # 719d
  nonce: 719d  # 719d
  message: "Drill-down confirmed the fix path is clean and small.\n\n**jvm_deps.py reference shape (186 LOC total).** Three top-level functions: `parse_gradle_dependencies`, `parse_maven_dependencies`, `parse_jvm_dependencies` (the umbrella). Each returns a `DependencyManifest` whose `entries: dict[str, dict]` maps module paths to `{\"direct\": True, ...}`. Consumer is `create_boundary_nodes` via the `dependency_manifest=...` kwarg (cli.py:6628).\n\n**`DependencyManifest.classify_import` is already language-agnostic** (supply_chain.py:70). Matching is longest-prefix on `/` or `.`. Go stdlib carve-out is \"first path segment has no dots AND path contains a slash.\" For Python we want an analogous stdlib carve-out via `sys.stdlib_module_names` (Python 3.10+) — single-word imports (`os`, `re`, `sys`, `pathlib`, etc.) are stdlib regardless of what's in pyproject.toml.\n\n**py_deps.py shape to build.** Parse order:\n\n1. `[project].dependencies` (PEP 621) — list of `\"name>=version\"` strings. Strip specifier.\n2. `[project.optional-dependencies]` (PEP 621 extras) — dict of name → list. Flatten to set of names; all tier-2.\n3. `[tool.poetry.dependencies]` (Poetry) — dict of name → version-spec. Exclude `python` key.\n4. `[tool.pdm.dev-dependencies]`, `[tool.hatch.envs.*.dependencies]` — cover other popular tools.\n5. Fallback: `requirements*.txt` if no `[project]` or `[tool.*]` hit.\n\n**Distribution-name → import-name mapping.** Not all dist names match import names:\n- `PyYAML` → import `yaml`\n- `beautifulsoup4` → import `bs4`\n- `ruamel.yaml` → import `ruamel.yaml` (matches)\n- `scikit-learn` → import `sklearn`\n- `pillow` → import `PIL`\n\nBest approach: use `importlib.metadata.packages_distributions()` at runtime, which returns `{import_name: [dist_names]}`. Invert for lookup. If the dev environment has the package installed (it usually does in a bakeoff context), this resolves correctly. Fallback: accept the dist name verbatim — most deps do match, and false-negatives just fall through to tier-3 (the current default, so no regression).\n\nFor hypergumbo's own pyproject.tomls the dist↔import names all match (`click`, `rich`, `hypothesis`, `ruamel.yaml`, etc.) with no hard mapping cases. So self-analysis will validate the mechanism before diverse-repo bakeoff stress-tests the mapping.\n\n**Net change footprint:**\n\n- New file: `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py_deps.py` (~100-150 LOC, parallel to jvm_deps.py)\n- `ir.py:702`: extend allow-list `language in (\"go\", \"java\", \"kotlin\", \"python\")`. Add `reason = \"direct dependency (pyproject.toml)\"` branch.\n- `cli.py` alongside `jvm_deps.parse_jvm_dependencies(...)` call: add `py_deps.parse_python_dependencies(...)` and merge manifests via `DependencyManifest.merge(...)` (already supported at supply_chain.py:118).\n- Tests in `packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/tests/test_py_deps.py`: PEP 621 fixture, Poetry fixture, hatch fixture, requirements.txt fallback fixture, stdlib carve-out, distribution-name mapping.\n\nTagging `awaits_bakeoff_validation` at merge time — the claim to validate is \"Python tier-2 node count on repos with pyproject.toml goes from 0 to (# direct deps).\"\n\n**Cross-reference to INV-miniz / WI-sikur / WI-fozoh.** This work consumes the same boundary-factory chokepoint WI-fozoh is structural-fix-expanding. The two should stack cleanly — py_deps.py lands first (narrow scope, new file), WI-fozoh fixes the file-placeholder externalization afterwards without colliding."  # 719d
